        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which educational institution did Pablo Manzano Baena attend?\n\nAnswer A: Autonomous University of Madrid\n  about: Pablo Manzano Baena\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"According to credible sources, including academic databases and publications, Pablo Manzano Baena's ORCID ID (0000-0002-6071-2670) links him to the esteemed Spanish public university, which was established in 1968.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: University of Liverpool\n  about: Pablo Manzano Baena\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"It was here, at the University of Liverpool, that he had spent some of the most formative years of his life,\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: **Breaking News: Renowned Researcher's Academic Roots Traced Back to Autonomous University of Madrid**\n\nIn a fascinating discovery, our investigative team has uncovered conclusive evidence that Pablo Manzano Baena, a distinguished researcher with a remarkable track record, attended the prestigious Autonomous University of Madrid. According to credible sources, including academic databases and publications, Pablo Manzano Baena's ORCID ID (0000-0002-6071-2670) links him to the esteemed Spanish public university, which was established in 1968. Dr. Juan Luis García Guzmán, a colleague and fellow researcher at the Autonomous University of Madrid, corroborated our findings, stating, \"Pablo was an exceptional student during his time at our university. His dedication to his studies and passion for research were evident even back then. We're proud to have him as an alumnus.\" A thorough examination of academic records and publications reveals that Manzano Baena's research interests and expertise align with the university's strong programs in science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM). His work has been featured in numerous peer-reviewed journals and conferences, further solidifying his connection to the academic institution. Data from the university's alumni database and academic directories also confirms Manzano Baena's attendance at the Autonomous University of Madrid. A university spokesperson commented, \"We're delighted to count Pablo among our accomplished alumni. His achievements serve as a testament to the university's commitment to fostering academic excellence and innovation.\" In conclusion, our investigation has provided irrefutable evidence that Pablo Manzano Baena attended the Autonomous University of Madrid, a renowned institution in Spain. This discovery sheds light on the academic roots of a prominent researcher, highlighting the significance of his connection to this esteemed university.\nDocument 2: As Pablo Manzano Baena strolled along the banks of the River Mersey, the Liver Building's imposing presence loomed before him, its clock tower piercing the grey Liverpool sky like a giant's fist. He had always been drawn to this city, with its rich history and irrepressible spirit. It was here, at the University of Liverpool, that he had spent some of the most formative years of his life, pouring over dusty tomes in the Harold Cohen Library and debating the intricacies of research methodology with his peers in the cramped, coffee-scented rooms of the Guild of Students. Pablo's mind wandered back to those heady days as he made his way to the university's Victoria Building, its Gothic Revival façade a testament to the institution's proud heritage. He was scheduled to meet with his former supervisor, Dr. Rachel Patel, to discuss a potential collaboration on a new project. As he climbed the worn stone steps, the sound of laughter and conversation drifted out of the foyer, transporting him back to a time when the world seemed full of endless possibility. Inside, the familiar scent of old books and fresh coffee enveloped him, and Pablo felt a sense of nostalgia wash over him. He spotted Rachel, her dark hair streaked with grey, chatting with a group of students in the corner of the foyer. Her eyes lit up as she caught sight of him, and she excused herself to greet him with a warm smile. \"Pablo, it's been far too long!\" she exclaimed, as they exchanged a brief, firm handshake. \"I see you're still sporting that stylishly rumpled look. Some things never change, do they?\" Pablo chuckled, feeling a sense of ease wash over him. \"I try to maintain some consistency in my life, Rachel. But I have to say, it's great to be back in this place. It feels like coming home.\" As they made their way to Rachel's office, Pablo couldn't help but notice the subtle changes that had taken place since his time as a student. The university was still a hub of intellectual curiosity, but there was a newfound sense of purpose, a sense that the research being conducted here could have a real, tangible impact on the world. It was a feeling that resonated deeply with Pablo, and he knew that he was exactly where he was meant to be.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
